    // EVERY vowel mark, so this could never fire on its own. Kept out rather than
    // left in as dead code that implies a check nobody is performing.
    { re: new RegExp('(^|[^' + AR + '])[\u0648\u0641]?(\u062E\u0630\u064A|\u062E\u0630|\u062A\u0641\u0636\u0644\u064A|\u062A\u0641\u0636\u0644|\u0627\u0633\u062A\u062E\u062F\u0645\u064A|\u0627\u0633\u062A\u062E\u062F\u0645|\u0627\u0637\u0644\u0628\u064A|\u0627\u0637\u0644\u0628|\u0623\u0643\u0645\u0644\u064A|\u0623\u0643\u0645\u0644|\u062C\u0631\u0628\u064A|\u062C\u0631\u0628)([^' + AR + ']|$)'), tag: 'informal_imperative' },
    // 2nd-person-FEMININE PRESENT - the أنتِ address in present tense.
    // Ruled wrong by Hussein 2026-08-04, after "يسعدنا حضرتك تستخدمي كود الخصم"
    // was generated, passed every check, and was DELIVERED to a real handset.
    // The imperative list could not catch it: استخدمي cannot match inside
    // تستخدمي because the boundary guard requires a non-Arabic character before
    // the marker, and ت precedes it. Same verb stems as the imperative list,
    // both the MSA form (تستخدمين) and the colloquial short form (تستخدمي).
    // Colloquial dropped-ن feminine present (تستخدمي). WRONG IN EVERY CASE,
    // including for a female customer: arabic-master-reference.md section 2 says that when a
    // form is dialectal, the standard MSA form is the one to use. This is the
    // exact shape that was generated, passed every check and was DELIVERED on
    // 2026-08-04 before rule 7 existed.
    { re: new RegExp('(^|[^' + AR + '])[\u0648\u0641]?(\u062a\u0633\u062a\u062e\u062f\u0645\u064a|\u062a\u0637\u0644\u0628\u064a|\u062a\u062c\u0631\u0628\u064a|\u062a\u0643\u0645\u0644\u064a|\u062a\u0623\u062e\u0630\u064a|\u062a\u062a\u0641\u0636\u0644\u064a)([^' + AR + ']|$)'), tag: 'colloquial_feminine_present' },
    // RULE 8 WAS HERE AND IS DELETED - ruled by Hussein 2026-09-08.
    // It required the ي carrier before an attached object pronoun
    // (تركتيهما, not تركتهما), ruled 2026-08-04 and narrowed to female-only
    // 2026-08-31. Both touches delivered on 2026-09-08 carried that spelling and
    // Hussein ruled it wrong: the ي form is DIALECT. The MSA 2nd-person feminine
    // past is bare with a final kasra (تركتِ), the masculine is bare (تركت), and
    // neither takes a ي - with or without a pronoun. Rule 10 below now owns every
    // case, so a marker that DEMANDED the wrong spelling could not be narrowed, only
    // removed. Kept as a comment because two live workflows shipped under it.
    // NO VOWEL MARKS. Ruled by Hussein 2026-08-04: commercial Arabic is written
    // unvowelled, so the unvowelled spellings in the style guide are the only
    // accepted ones. This also removes a genuine contradiction - rule 2 flags
    // ت + kasra, which is exactly the vowelled spelling of اخترت,
